<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shafi'i school</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Shafi'i</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Shafi'i school is one of the four major schools of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Islamic law, named after the jurist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-shafii">al-Shafi'i</a>. It became widespread in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizamiyya">Nizamiyya</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a> institutionalised Shafi'i jurisprudence, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a> followed Shafi'i and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a> law in its legal administration.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The school belongs to the Ahl al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">Hadith</a> tradition and takes its name from the jurist and traditionist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-shafii">al-Shafi'i</a>, who lived from about 767 to 820 CE. He visited most of the great centres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-jurisprudence">Islamic jurisprudence</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> and gathered a comprehensive knowledge of different legal theories. He was a student of Malik ibn Anas, the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki school</a>, and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-shaybani">Muhammad Shaybani</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi</a> scholar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. His students first spread his thought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and Baghdad, and by the 10th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> had also become chief centres of it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-shafii">Al-Shafi'i</a> was the first jurist to insist that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a> were the decisive source of law over the traditional doctrines of earlier thinkers. In order of priority the sources are the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>, the Sunnah, defined by him as the sayings, acts and tacit acquiescence of Muhammad as related in solidly established traditions, then analogical reasoning, qiyas, and finally ijma, the consensus of scholars, which was accepted but not stressed. The school rejected dependence on local community practice as a source of precedent and rebuffed personal opinion and juristic discretion. The groundwork text is al-Shafi'i's al-Risala, composed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. A first version, produced during his stay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, is lost.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The school later held the judgeships exclusively in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, Kirman, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, and it flourished in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daylam">Daylam</a>. The Ghurids endorsed it in the 11th and 12th centuries. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Salah al-Din</a> it again became the paramount school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, after a period of Shia influence, and it was the official school of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid dynasty</a> and remained prominent in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> period. The rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in the 16th century replaced Shafi'i judges with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi</a> scholars, and after the start of Safavid rule its presence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> was limited to the western regions.</p><p>Traders and merchants spread it across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. It is predominant in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, Malaysia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, and among most Kurdish Muslims. It is the third-largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> school, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shafi'i school” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Shafi'i</b> jurisprudence predominated at the Ayyubid court, though the dynasty also endowed institutions for the other Sunni schools.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Islam of the <b>Shafi'i school</b> became central to court and urban life, while Brunei's brass-casters became famous across the region for their cannon and vessels.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brunei-sultanate">Brunei Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Many highlanders of Ghor had followed the Karramiyya, a pietist Sunni movement, but Ghiyath al-Din Muhammad later adopted the <b>Shafi'i school</b> while Mu'izz al-Din became a Hanafi, and the theologian Fakhr al-Din al-Razi was active at Ghurid courts.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghurid-sultanate">Ghurid Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Religiously the country divides between Zaydi Shia Muslims in the northern highlands and <b>Shafi'i</b> Sunni Muslims in the south, along the coastal plain and in the east.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-yemen">Republic of Yemen · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Nizamiyya madrasas in Baghdad, Nishapur, Isfahan and elsewhere promoted <b>Shafi'i</b> jurisprudence and Ash'ari theology, and helped to standardise Sunni learning and legal practice across the empire's diverse territories.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…such as Ibn Hajar al-Haytami, Nazifi and Qastallani wrote commentaries on it, over 90 in all, and the <b>Shafi'i</b> hadith master Ibn Hajar al-Asqalani (d. 852 AH) studied it, reading it aloud to his teacher and receiving it in writing from a transmitter who had heard it from…</blockquote><a href="/songs/al-burda">Al-Burda</a></li></ul></section>
